(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-blocks-ventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Arrhythmias II: Blocks and Ventriculars Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 72-year-old man with congestive heart failure and an ejection fraction of 20% is in stable, sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia with a pulse. Which intravenous antiarrhythmic should be avoided?","choices":["Procainamide","Lidocaine","Amiodarone","Magnesium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This man has congestive heart failure with an ejection fraction of 20%, and procainamide is to be avoided with a prolonged QT or congestive heart failure. His stable, sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia can still be pharmacologically cardioverted with intravenous lidocaine or amiodarone. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man takes no medications and has never had a heart attack. A routine electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.25 seconds with every beat conducted, and he asks why his heart is doing this. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["A silent tick-borne infection","Hidden damage from a heart attack","An overactive vagus nerve","Age-related wear of the pathway"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His constant PR of 0.25 seconds with every beat conducted is first-degree block. He takes no offending drugs (beta blockers, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, digoxin) and has had no infarction, so at 81 the explanation is the most common cause: age-related degenerative change of the conduction system. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with stable coronary artery disease, who has never had a heart attack, asks whether he is at any risk of sudden cardiac death. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Risk rises mainly after age 80","Risk exists even without an infarction","Risk begins after an infarction","Inherited rhythms are the main risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This man has coronary artery disease, the most common predisposing condition for sudden cardiac death, and that holds even without a history of myocardial infarction. Sudden cardiac death is unexpected cardiac death within 1 hour of collapse, and an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) is the device used to prevent it. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 27-year-old graduate student drinks several energy drinks a day and has occasional skipped beats. A monitor shows isolated premature ventricular contractions; his echocardiogram and electrolytes are normal. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Start lifelong amiodarone","Cut caffeine and stimulants","Stop exercising","Avoid dairy products"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His energy drinks supply caffeine and stimulants, named triggers of premature ventricular contractions, and his normal echocardiogram and electrolytes argue against structural or electrolyte causes. Low-risk patients are reassured and helped to eliminate triggers; a beta blocker is first-line only if symptoms persist. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man has fatigue, shortness of breath on exertion and palpitations. His electrocardiogram shows every P wave conducted to a narrow QRS with a constant PR interval of 0.34 seconds. No offending drug or reversible cause is found. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassurance and follow-up","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous magnesium","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His fatigue, shortness of breath and palpitations fit symptomatic first-degree block, and his PR of 0.34 seconds is above the 0.3-second threshold. With no offending drug or reversible cause, a permanent pacemaker is considered for symptomatic patients or a PR greater than 0.3 seconds. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man returns from a camping trip in Connecticut with fatigue and an expanding red rash around a tick bite on his thigh. His electrocardiogram shows a constant PR interval of 0.30 seconds with every P wave conducted. Which infection should be suspected as the cause of his conduction delay?","choices":["Lyme disease","Infective endocarditis","Chagas disease","Viral pharyngitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A tick bite with an expanding red rash after camping in Connecticut points to Lyme disease, a named cause of first-degree atrioventricular block. His constant PR of 0.30 seconds with every P wave conducted is first-degree block, which is most often asymptomatic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has a regular wide QRS tachycardia at 190 per minute with identical complexes. He is confused, his blood pressure is 72/40, and a carotid pulse is palpable. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation","Intravenous amiodarone","Transcutaneous pacing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular wide tachycardia at 190 with identical complexes is monomorphic ventricular tachycardia. His confusion and blood pressure of 72/40 (altered mental status and hypotension) define instability, and with a carotid pulse present, the treatment is synchronized cardioversion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman taking a loop diuretic for heart failure has new palpitations felt in her neck. Telemetry shows frequent premature ventricular contractions. Which laboratory values should be checked first?","choices":["Glucose and lactate","Potassium and magnesium","Thyroid hormone and cortisol","Troponin and lipase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Electrolyte imbalance (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, hypercalcemia) and hypoxemia are the named causes of premature ventricular contractions, and her loop diuretic puts potassium and magnesium at risk. Palpitations felt in the neck are the classic symptom of these beats, which occur with or without structural heart disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man is found unresponsive and pulseless. The monitor shows a flat line in two leads with no P waves or QRS complexes. Compressions are underway. Which drug is a mainstay of treatment?","choices":["Epinephrine","Amiodarone","Lidocaine","Magnesium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A flat line in two leads with no P waves or QRS complexes is asystole, which is unshockable. High-quality chest compressions and epinephrine 1 mg (1:10,000) by intravenous or intraosseous push every 3 to 5 minutes are the mainstays of treatment, and early intubation is considered. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has a week of fatigue and dizziness. Her heart rate is 36. The electrocardiogram shows regular P waves at 80 per minute and regular wide QRS complexes at 36 per minute, with no consistent relationship between them; some P waves sit inside T waves. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mobitz type II block","Mobitz type I block","Ventricular tachycardia","Complete heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Regular P waves at 80 and regular wide QRS complexes at 36 with no relationship, and P waves buried in T waves, are two separate rhythms: third-degree (complete) block, in which no P waves conduct. The ventricular rate is usually under 40, and her dizziness and fatigue fit; she needs pacing, then a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man survives an out-of-hospital cardiac arrest from pulseless ventricular tachycardia. After stabilization no reversible cause is found. Which device is most appropriate before discharge?","choices":["Implantable loop recorder","Biventricular pacemaker","Implantable defibrillator","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He survived cardiac arrest from pulseless ventricular tachycardia with no reversible cause, so once stabilized he needs an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) for secondary prevention of sudden cardiac death related to life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a right bundle branch block found on routine testing. He has no symptoms, and his examination is normal. Which approach to treatment is correct?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Antiarrhythmic drug therapy","Catheter ablation","Care of the underlying condition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Right bundle branch block is usually asymptomatic, and its treatment depends on the underlying condition, such as ischemic heart disease or pulmonary hypertension. New block is not always pathological and may occur with no apparent heart disease, so this silent man needs care of any underlying condition, not a device or drug. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman is in asystolic cardiac arrest, and resuscitation continues. Her family asks about her chances. Which statement about this rhythm is most accurate?","choices":["A shock should restore it","Better than fibrillation","Recovery is expected","Prognosis is poor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asystole, like pulseless electrical activity, carries a poor prognosis and is usually an end-of-life rhythm. It is unshockable, so resuscitation relies on high-quality compressions and epinephrine; ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation are the arrest rhythms with the best prognosis. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has fatigue and ankle swelling. A 24-hour monitor shows premature ventricular contractions in 22% of beats, and his echocardiogram shows a newly reduced ejection fraction; coronary angiography and other tests find no other cause. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Catheter ablation","Reassurance and follow-up","Digoxin","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A burden of 22% (over 15%) and a newly reduced ejection fraction with no other cause make this isolated premature ventricular contraction-induced cardiomyopathy, a high-risk group. With heart failure signs and symptoms (fatigue, ankle swelling), the treatment is catheter ablation. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman is scheduled for an implantable cardioverter defibrillator. She asks what the device will do inside her chest. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["Release antiarrhythmic drugs","Replace her heart medications","Detect and shock fatal rhythms","Slowly dissolve artery plaque"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) is used to prevent sudden cardiac death from arrhythmias: its sensing electrodes detect fatal arrhythmias and it delivers a shock. Newer devices can also act as pacemakers, and a wearable (external) version, the LifeVest, also exists. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with hypertension and diabetes for 20 years is found to have Mobitz type II block. He takes no rate-slowing drugs and has had no infections. He asks what caused it. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["His blood pressure tablets","A recent viral illness","Conduction system wear","An overactive vagus nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. At 74, with 20 years of hypertension and diabetes and no drugs or infections to blame, his Mobitz type II comes from the most common cause: acquired disease of the His-Purkinje system, especially degenerative change from old age, chronic hypertension and diabetes. The definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with known pulmonary sarcoidosis faints at work. She takes no medications. Her heart rate is 35, and the electrocardiogram shows P waves at a regular rate with no relationship to slow QRS complexes. Which named cause of her block fits her best?","choices":["Granulomatous infiltration","Increased vagal tone","Age-related degeneration","A medication side effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her rate of 35 with P waves unrelated to the QRS complexes is complete block, and her syncope matters because it carries an increased risk of sudden death. Inflammatory and infiltrative disease (myocarditis, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis) is a named cause, and her known sarcoidosis, a granulomatous disease, is the fit at 48. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman is brought in after fainting at home. She is confused, her heart rate is 34 and her blood pressure is 78/40. The electrocardiogram shows regular P waves, a constant PR interval in the conducted beats and many P waves with no QRS after them. Medications given so far have not raised her rate. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Observation on a monitor","Transcutaneous pacing","Synchronized cardioversion","Defibrillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Regular P waves, a constant PR in conducted beats and dropped QRS complexes are Mobitz type II, and her confusion, hypotension and rate of 34 make her unstable. Emergent options are atropine, epinephrine and transcutaneous pacing; with drugs failing, transcutaneous pacing bridges her to a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman in ventricular fibrillation arrest has received shocks, epinephrine and amiodarone, and she remains in ventricular fibrillation. Which drug may be considered next?","choices":["Metoprolol","Adenosine","Lidocaine","Verapamil"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. She has had shocks, epinephrine and amiodarone and is still in ventricular fibrillation, so a lidocaine push may be considered for rhythm refractory to shock after the antiarrhythmic. Calcium gluconate and sodium bicarbonate may also be considered while cardiopulmonary resuscitation continues. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man who grew up in rural Bolivia has months of fatigue and exertional breathlessness. His heart rate is 38 and the electrocardiogram shows P waves completely dissociated from slow, wide QRS complexes. Which infection is a named cause of his conduction disease?","choices":["Lyme disease","Malaria","Tuberculosis","Chagas disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A heart rate of 38 with P waves dissociated from slow, wide QRS complexes is complete block, and Chagas disease is a named cause. A Latin American parasitic infection, it fits a man raised in rural Bolivia with months of fatigue and exertional breathlessness. Definitive treatment is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man in cardiac arrest remains in ventricular fibrillation despite repeated shocks, continuous cardiopulmonary resuscitation and epinephrine. Which antiarrhythmic is given next?","choices":["Digoxin","Amiodarone","Adenosine","Diltiazem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If ventricular fibrillation or pulseless ventricular tachycardia persists after shocks, cardiopulmonary resuscitation and epinephrine, amiodarone 300 mg is given by rapid intravenous or intraosseous push, and a 150 mg dose can follow. Lidocaine may be considered if the rhythm stays refractory after the antiarrhythmic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with a long-standing left bundle branch block now has dizziness, shortness of breath and dyspnea on exertion that resemble a heart failure flare. What does the onset of symptoms from the block usually indicate?","choices":["A drug side effect","An accessory pathway","Severe heart disease","A harmless variant"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients with left bundle branch block are asymptomatic. Those who develop symptoms, like her dizziness, shortness of breath and dyspnea on exertion resembling a heart failure flare, usually have severe underlying cardiac disease and do not tolerate the block well. Prognosis depends on that disease. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man on telemetry develops a run of identical wide QRS complexes at 180 per minute. Within 18 seconds his blood pressure falls to 70/40 and he loses consciousness, and the team shocks him back to sinus rhythm. How should this episode be classified?","choices":["Ventricular fibrillation","Ventricular bigeminy","Sustained ventricular tachycardia","Non-sustained ventricular tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular tachycardia is sustained if it lasts 30 seconds or more or causes hemodynamic collapse in under 30 seconds. His run lasted only 18 seconds, but his pressure fell to 70/40 and he lost consciousness, so it counts as sustained; the identical complexes make it monomorphic. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with ischemic cardiomyopathy has had a regular wide QRS tachycardia at 160 per minute, with identical complexes, for 10 minutes. She is alert and talking, her blood pressure is 118/74, and she has no chest pain or shortness of breath. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous adenosine","Intravenous antiarrhythmic","Immediate defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 118/74 and no chest pain or dyspnea, so her sustained monomorphic ventricular tachycardia is hemodynamically stable. After a monitor, intravenous access, a 12-lead electrocardiogram, oxygen and labs, it is cardioverted with intravenous lidocaine, amiodarone or procainamide (avoided in heart failure). — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman on telemetry has torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). The first-line intravenous push broke the first run, but short runs keep recurring. She is alert with a pulse and a normal blood pressure. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Magnesium infusion","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous procainamide","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades de pointes, and more magnesium is then given as an infusion. The push broke her first run, so with short runs recurring in an alert, normotensive patient, the next step is a magnesium infusion; amiodarone, procainamide and beta blockers are avoided. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man on telemetry has a prolonged QT after starting a new medication and then develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (QRS complexes that vary in shape and twist around the baseline). He becomes unresponsive and pulseless. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Transcutaneous pacing","Defibrillation","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous magnesium first"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Polymorphic ventricular tachycardia with a prolonged QT is torsades de pointes, and a hemodynamically unstable patient, here unresponsive and pulseless, is treated with defibrillation (unsynchronized cardioversion). Magnesium is the drug for torsades in a conscious patient; once he is pulseless, the shock comes first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension is told that a routine electrocardiogram shows a long-standing left bundle branch block, unchanged from tracings years ago. He feels well and has a normal ejection fraction. He asks whether it needs treatment. Which answer is most accurate?","choices":["He needs a pacemaker now","He needs a defibrillator","He needs catheter ablation","He needs no therapy for it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. His left bundle branch block is long-standing, unchanged from old tracings and silent, with a normal ejection fraction, so it is left untreated. Only symptomatic block with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction gets a biventricular pacer, and a new block, unlike his, is treated as a sign of pathology. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with five days of vomiting and diarrhea has a prolonged QT and brief runs of torsades de pointes (QRS complexes that twist around the baseline). Which electrolyte disturbances should be sought as causes of her long QT?","choices":["Low potassium, low calcium","High potassium, high calcium","Low sodium, high chloride","High sodium, low phosphate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Five days of vomiting and diarrhea set up electrolyte losses, and hypokalemia and hypocalcemia are named causes of acquired long-QT syndrome, which accounts for most torsades de pointes. Drugs are the other major acquired cause, so her medication list deserves a look too. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman taking metoprolol and diltiazem for palpitations has a new rhythm in which the PR interval lengthens until a QRS is dropped. She has mild fatigue. Which part of her history is a named cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Her mild fatigue","Her female sex","Her rate-slowing drugs","Her history of palpitations"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A PR that lengthens until a QRS drops is Mobitz type I (Wenckebach), and beta blockers and nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers, with digoxin, are named causes. She takes metoprolol, a beta blocker, and diltiazem, a nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, so her drugs are the first place to look. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman five days after hip replacement develops sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain and a heart rate of 118. Her electrocardiogram shows a new right bundle branch block. Which acute cause of the block should be suspected?","choices":["Hypertensive crisis","Pulmonary embolism","Aortic dissection","Pericardial effusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A sudden increase in right ventricular pressure from a pulmonary embolism is a named acute cause of right bundle branch block. Five days after hip replacement, with sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain and a heart rate of 118, she fits that picture, and treatment of the block depends on the underlying condition. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 31"}]};
